    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d538">

                <pb facs="0726" n="612" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg612"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.51/1465</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hood</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-03-09"><hi rendition="#italic">March 9,
                        1922</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received April 8.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 32</seg>
                    <lb/>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Diplomatic</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith for
                    the information of the Department a certain confidential communication dated
                    February, 23, 1922,<anchor n="10" xml:id="d538-f1"/> from H. F. Worley, Esquire,
                    General Receiver of Customs and Financial Adviser of Liberia, relative to
                    certain large orders for materials and implements being placed in England and
                    the United States on Account of the Liberian Frontier Force.</p>
                <p>With reference to this confidential communication it is understood by the
                    Legation that the General Receiver of Customs and Financial Adviser desires the
                    Department’s advice on the matter of payment for said materials and
                    implements.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Solomon Porter
                    Hood</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
